   859                              <1> ; ---- machine code: the grave -----------------------------------------------
   860                              <1> ; '`' plus a hex digit loads bytes: ( addr -- addr' ). Pairs of hex digits
   861                              <1> ; are stored from addr until a non-hex character; then a RET (C3h) is
   862                              <1> ; written and its address is left. An odd last digit is consumed and its
   863                              <1> ; byte is overwritten by the RET. (Never range-checked: the spec says so.)
   864                              <1> ; '`' plus anything else calls the machine code at TOS: ( addr -- ).
   865                              <1> ;
   866                              <1> ; The machine-code contract: the code is entered by CALL, with the return
   867                              <1> ; address at [ESP] and the data stack above it: [ESP+4] is the top cell
   868                              <1> ; (the one under the address, which was consumed), [ESP+8] the next, one
   869                              <1> ; dword per cell, the cell in the low word. EBX holds the code's own
   870                              <1> ; address (ARENA + addr), and its high word is the arena's (0041h): a
   871                              <1> ; B8Script address a is the byte at ARENA + a. The code may push and pop
   872                              <1> ; dwords, and may change EAX, EBX, ECX, EDX and the flags; it must keep
   873                              <1> ; ESI, EDI and EBP, leave the direction flag clear, and return with RET
   874                              <1> ; (C3h, which the loader writes). On return the dword on top of the stack
   875                              <1> ; becomes TOS. Only the low word of each cell counts: the interpreter puts
   876                              <1> ; the arena's high word back into every cell on the stack.
   877                              <1> grave1: CALLP   hexc
   877                              <2> grave1: 
